# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages and Netlify (both read this file).
#
# NOTE: this site is currently served by GitHub Pages, which ignores this file
# entirely - it cannot set custom headers at all. On that deployment the same
# headers are applied at the edge by a Cloudflare response header transform
# rule, kept in cloudflare/response-headers.json. If you change anything here,
# change it there too, or the two deployments stop agreeing.
#
# This file is kept because it is the whole configuration if the site ever moves
# to a host that reads it, and because it documents the intent in one place.
#
# These are defence in depth. The Content-Security-Policy inside each page's
# <meta> tag already carries the load-bearing rules; frame-ancestors is the one
# directive a meta tag cannot express, so it has to live here.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), usb=(), payment=()

# camera=(self) rather than camera=(): /qr-barcode-reader/ reads a code from a
# live camera, and a page cannot ask for one that its own site has switched off.
# It is the narrowest form that permits it - this origin only, no embedded
# frame, and no other capability moved - and every other tool here is unaffected
# because none of them asks. If that tool ever goes, put camera=() back.
#
# Deliberately NOT set: Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy. Enabling it would block
# the cross-origin <img> loads that "Add from a web address" depends on.
